--TEST--
A literal beside a generic parameter takes the type the bindings gave that parameter, in either argument order -- `rule:types/conversion` and `rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:types/single-value-types`, `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// findings.md's D33. A generic call binds its type variables before it checks
// its arguments, so a literal at a position only substitution closes has
// nothing to be placed against on the first pass -- and `2` beside a `uint`
// was `E0401: expected uint, found int`. `check_generic_args` in
// `crates/nvs-types/src/expr/args.rs` owns the rule and why it is placement
// rather than assignability.

uint $u = 2;

// Both orders, because the two fail differently without the rule: written
// second the literal is compared against the `uint` the first argument bound,
// and written first it would bind `T` to `int` and refuse the `uint`.
Core\Test::assertSame($u, 2);
Core\Test::assertSame(2, $u);

// The half of `uint`'s range a literal cannot reach on its own: above
// `i64::MAX` it is legal only where a `uint` is expected, and a parameter
// that binds to one is such a place.
uint $big = 18446744073709551615;
Core\Test::assertSame($big, 18446744073709551615);
echo "a uint parameter places an integer literal in either order\n";

// The rule is placement, not a `uint` special case, so every other position a
// literal takes its type from reaches a generic parameter too: `rule:types/arithmetic`'s
// fractional literal at `decimal`, `rule:types/single-value-types`'s string atom, and `rule:types/grammar`
// 's two `bool` singletons.
decimal $d = 1.50;
Core\Test::assertSame($d, 1.50);
string $s = "novis";
Core\Test::assertSame($s, "novis");
bool $b = true;
Core\Test::assertSame($b, true);
echo "so does a decimal, a string and a bool\n";

// A literal is evidence about a type variable only where nothing written
// binds it -- and it still is where the literals are all there is: `T` here
// comes from `7` and from nothing else.
array<int> $filled = Core\Arr::fill(2, 7);
Core\Test::assertSame($filled, [7, 7]);
echo "a literal still binds a variable nothing else binds\n";

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: `assertSame` throws on a
// mismatch, so what this guards is a placement that stops being reached at
// all -- each block adds its own rows, and a compile that refuses any one of
// them never gets here.
int $placed = 3 + 3 + 1;
echo "placed=", $placed, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a uint parameter places an integer literal in either order
so does a decimal, a string and a bool
a literal still binds a variable nothing else binds
placed=7
